    {
      "abstract": "Abstract The development of floating wind energy is necessary to expand offshore wind energy to deeper waters. From a control perspective, floating wind turbines pose interesting new challenges. Among these challenges is the mitigation of wave disturbances, which occur above the bandwidth of the collective pitch feedback controller. Therefore, wave feedforward control has been proposed, which uses a radar to estimate a preview of the wave loads acting on the platform. To obtain optimally calibrated wave feedforward controllers of user-defined complexity, this work proposes a data-driven approach that integrates model-based insights with the iterative feedback tuning (IFT) algorithm. The approach balances rotor speed tracking and platform pitching motion in its performance criterion. To assess the performance of the proposed approach, the frequency response of the optimal wave feedforward controller is derived and identified, and the IFT calibrated controllers are compared to this controller. It is shown that the method is effective in calibrating the controller to a trade off between mitigating platform pitching motion, and rotor speed tracking, and it is demonstrated that the data-driven controller approaches the performance of the optimal controller.",
