    {
      "abstract": "Abstract The simulation of rotor-wake interactions in floating offshore wind turbines will provide invaluable insight for the development of future floating wind farms. In fact, similarly to their fixed-bottom counterparts, floating wind turbines will be installed in clusters to reduce installation and O&M costs. In this framework, Free Vortex Wake (FVW) methods can simulate rotor-wake interactions at a fraction of the cost of CFD methods. However, the capabilities and limitations of this method when investigating rotor-wake interactions in floating offshore wind turbines are still unclear. In this study, experimental wind tunnel data on tandem floating wind turbines under imposed pitch and yaw motion is leveraged to investigate the reliability of the FVW method. The impact of inflow turbulence and main simulation parameters on the results was investigated, providing valuable insight for future studies on rotor-wake interactions of FOWTs. Results showed that the FVW method requires significant tuning to correctly capture rotor-wake interactions, as input parameters may drastically affect the reliability of the simulations. Nevertheless, after a careful tuning campaign, the FVW method can provide valuable insight into the mean power output and unsteady loads of a downstream machine.",
