      "abstract": "Abstract The accurate experimental simulation of the dynamic coupling between platform motion and turbine performance is critical for the development of Floating Offshore Wind Turbines (FOWTs). While Hardware-in-the-Loop (HIL) hybrid testing offers a promising solution to the inherent conflict between Froude and Reynolds scaling laws, significant challenges remain regarding real-time numerical stability and the contamination of aerodynamic measurements by inertial loads. In this study, a novel physical-numerical coupled model system is developed and validated against OpenFAST simulations to address these issues. A new similarity criterion based on dynamic aerodynamic similarity is applied, employing model blades designed with thrust similarity to resolve performance discrepancies at low Reynolds numbers. This approach is integrated with a method that utilizes dynamic thrust fitted from pressure data to eliminate the interference of inertial forces. Furthermore, a corrected convolution scheme for radiation forces is proposed to ensure numerical accuracy and efficiency under the large time steps inherent to hybrid testing. Results showed that the proposed system achieves high fidelity in capturing the platform’s kinematic response, with aerodynamic loads determining the mean equilibrium and wave loads driving high-frequency oscillations. This methodology demonstrates superior performance in balancing real-time capability with accuracy, providing a reliable framework for future hybrid model tests.",
